# tests/offsetskip/offset_skip.rxt — [OPT-K], the OFFSET-k CANDIDATE-START SKIP
# (docs/design/offset_k_skip.md).
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR, and what it deliberately is not. The mechanism is
# ANSWER-IDENTITY-preserving by construction, so its own correctness argument
# is a proof (the note's §3.2: every test is a NECESSARY condition of a match
# beginning at that position) and its own gate is `make test-axes` under
# `-fno-offset-skip`, which compares the WHOLE corpus case by case across the
# axis. This file is neither of those. It is the ARITHMETIC of the emitted
# skip, exercised where the arithmetic can be wrong:
#
#   §1  the worked examples' k-sets fire at all
#   §2  the hit -> start mapping at the SUBJECT START (`cand = hit - k*`
#       cannot underflow, and a match at offset 0 must still be found)
#   §3  the `cand + maxk >= n` guard at the SUBJECT END (a truncated tail
#       must not be read past, and a match that ends exactly at n must not
#       be lost)
#   §4  OVERLAPPING candidates and the resume (`pos = cand + 1`): a failed
#       candidate must not skip the real one one byte later
#   §5  RESTARTS (`ms <P>`), which is what a find-all loop is made of
#   §6  the RESEED — the `\b` cell the note's §2.1 and §5.4 are about, where
#       losing the word context of `s[cand-1]` is a FALSE MATCH
#   §7  the NEGATIVE examples: patterns the selection declines, kept here so
#       that a change which starts selecting them fails a case that names
#       them rather than moving silently
#
# ORACLE: python3 `re` (CLAUDE.md's base tier), via
# `tests/harness/verify_rxt.py`. Every expectation below was written by
# running that oracle, not by reading the emitter. No `\Z` appears here, so
# `tests/assertions/absolute.rxt`'s oracle carve-out does not apply.
#
# A NOTE ON WHY THE PATTERNS ARE THE SHAPES THEY ARE. Each §1-§6 block is a
# pattern the selection ACCEPTS — checked by tests/codegen/run_offset_skip.sh
# against the artifact, since a case here passes just as well on a pattern
# that gets no skip at all, and a corpus that silently stopped exercising the
# mechanism is this project's most-recorded check failure. The two files name
# the same patterns on purpose.

# ---- §1 the worked examples ------------------------------------------------

# `iso-ts`'s prefix: digits at 0-3, `-` at 4, digits at 5-6, `-` at 7.
# Selected k-set `0,4*` — the scan is memchr('-') at offset 4.
pattern \d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}
features classes
m "2026-08-28" 0 10
m "log line 2026-08-28 ok" 9 19
n "2026-08-2 ok"
n "202-08-28"
n "no digits at all here"
# a `-` at offset 4 with nothing else right: the scan fires, the verify
# refuses, and the whole subject must still come back NOMATCH
n "abcd-ef-gh"
n "abcd-01-23"

# `uuid`'s head: `-` at 8 and at 13, both scanned/verified, `\b` at the front.
pattern \b[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}
features classes,assertions
m "deadbeef-cafe" 0 13
m "id=deadbeef-cafe;" 3 16
n "deadbeef-cafx"
n "deadbee-cafe"
# the leading `\b`: a word character to the LEFT forbids the match, and this
# is the cell §6 is about one pattern over
n "xdeadbeef-cafe"

# `stack-frame`'s head: `a` at 0, `t` at 1, ` ` at 2. The scan is memchr('t')
# at offset 1, so `cand = hit - 1`.
pattern \bat [a-z]
features assertions
m "at x" 0 4
m "look at y" 5 9
n "cat x"
n "atx"
n "at  "

# a long literal: the scan moves to the rarest byte the prior knows, `X` at
# offset 6, and offsets 0..5 and 7..9 are what the verify chain is built from
pattern needleXYZW
m "needleXYZW" 0 10
m "..needleXYZW.." 2 12
n "needleXYZv"
n "XYZW"

# ---- §2 the hit -> start mapping at the SUBJECT START -----------------------
#
# `cand = hit - k*` with the match at offset 0, so `hit` is exactly `k*` and
# the subtraction lands on 0. The scan begins at `pos + k*`, which is the
# first position it may legally look at.

pattern \d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}
features classes
m "2026-08-28 and more" 0 10

pattern needleXYZW
m "needleXYZW tail" 0 10

# one byte of lead-in, so `cand` is 1 and the underflow guard is exercised
# from the other side
pattern needleXYZW
m ".needleXYZW" 1 11

# ---- §3 the `cand + maxk >= n` guard at the SUBJECT END ---------------------
#
# The skip declines any candidate without `maxk + 1` bytes left, which is
# sound because the walk stopped before the pattern could accept. These cases
# pin both directions: a truncated tail must be NOMATCH, and a match ending
# exactly at `n` must still be found.

pattern \d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}
features classes
# ends exactly at n
m "x 2026-08-28" 2 12
# `-` present at offset 4 of a candidate whose tail is cut short
n "2026-08-2"
n "2026-0"
n "2026-"
n "2026"
n "-"
n ""

pattern \b[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}
features classes,assertions
m "zz deadbeef-cafe" 3 16
n "deadbeef-caf"
n "deadbeef-"
n "deadbeef"

# ---- §4 overlapping candidates and the resume ------------------------------
#
# `pos = cand + 1` after a failed candidate. A resume of `cand + 1 + k*`
# instead — the obvious off-by-one, and tests/mech row S185 — would skip a
# real match closer than `k*` to a failed candidate. The rows immediately
# below EXERCISE the resume (each has candidates that fail their verify and a
# match found later), and MEASURABLY DO NOT DETECT that plant; the block after
# them says why and carries the row that does.

pattern \d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}
features classes
# hyphens at 4 and 5: the first candidate fails, the second is the match
m "1234-2026-08-28" 5 15
# a run of hyphens before the real one
m "----2026-08-28" 4 14
# two failing candidates then a good one
m "aaaa-bb-cc 0000-11-22" 11 21
# the real match's own `-` at offset 4 is ALSO a failing candidate's offset-4
# byte one position earlier
m "x2026-08-28" 1 11

pattern needleXYZW
# a near-miss sharing the scan byte `X` at a nearby offset
m "needleXYZv needleXYZW" 11 21
m "XXXXXXneedleXYZW" 6 16

# THE RESUME'S OFF-BY-ONE IS UNREACHABLE ON EVERY PATTERN ABOVE, AND THAT WAS
# MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Planting `pos = cand + 1 + k*` in the emitter
# leaves this whole file at 75/75 GREEN (tests/mech S185's harness arm, and
# the VALIDATION RECORD in tests/codegen/run_offset_skip.sh). The reason is
# structural: the plant loses a match only if a real start `p` lies in
# `(cand, cand + k*]`, which puts the DECOY's scan byte at match offset
# `h - p < k*` — so the pattern must ALLOW its own scan byte somewhere before
# the offset it is scanned at. None of the four above does: `uuid` and
# `iso-ts` have hex and digits before their `-`, `stack-frame` has `a` before
# its `t`, `needleXYZW` has `needle` before its `X`.
#
# So the file gets a pattern that DOES allow it, and it is the only row here
# that turns that off-by-one into a wrong answer. `[-a]{3}-b` scans for `-` at
# offset 3 and permits `-` at offsets 0-2.
pattern [-a]{3}-b
# "zaa--b": the `-` at index 3 is a candidate at 0, whose offset-0 byte `z`
# fails the verify; the real match starts at 1, one position later. Resuming
# at `cand + 1` finds it; resuming at `cand + 1 + 3` runs off the end.
m "zaa--b" 1 6
m "zzaaa-b" 2 7
m "z-aa-b" 1 6
n "zaa-b"
n "zzzz--b"

pattern \bat [a-z]
features assertions
# `t` at several offsets, only one of which is a candidate start. The first
# draft of these two rows expected a match in "tttat x" and "attat q" and the
# ORACLE REFUSED BOTH: the `a` at offset 3 is preceded by a word character, so
# `\b` is false there and neither subject matches at all. Corrected to
# subjects whose decoy `t`s sit before a real boundary — which is the stronger
# case anyway, since a row that expects NO match cannot tell a skip that
# overshot from a pattern that simply did not match.
m "tt at x" 3 7
m "xat at y" 4 8

# ---- §5 restarts, which is what a find-all loop is made of ------------------
#
# `ms <P>` is one search from `startpos = P`, i.e. exactly one iteration of
# the find-all loop. These walk a subject with three matches the way that
# loop would, and then ask for one past the last.

pattern \d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}
features classes
m "2026-08-28 2026-08-29 2026-08-30" 0 10
ms 10 "2026-08-28 2026-08-29 2026-08-30" 11 21
ms 21 "2026-08-28 2026-08-29 2026-08-30" 22 32
ns 32 "2026-08-28 2026-08-29 2026-08-30"
# a restart landing INSIDE what would have been a candidate
ms 1 "2026-08-28 2026-08-29" 11 21
ms 5 "2026-08-28 2026-08-29" 11 21

pattern needleXYZW
m "needleXYZW needleXYZW" 0 10
ms 10 "needleXYZW needleXYZW" 11 21
ns 21 "needleXYZW needleXYZW"

# ---- §6 the RESEED --------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE CELL THE MECHANISM IS MOST LIKELY TO GET WRONG. `\b`'s truth reads the
# byte to the LEFT, which pcrec carries in the DFA state's IDENTITY, and the
# offset-k skip jumps over the very bytes that were carrying it (the note's
# §2.1). Landing in the no-context start state would evaluate `\b` as TRUE
# after a word character and MATCH — so every `n` below is a FALSE MATCH if
# the reseed is dropped, and `tests/mech` has that as a sabotage row.

pattern \b[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}
features classes,assertions
m "12-34" 0 5
m " 12-34" 1 6
m ".12-34" 1 6
# a word character immediately to the left: NOMATCH, and the skip must not
# jump the machine past it into a clean start state
n "a12-34"
n "z12-34"
# (the first draft wrote "912-34-56" here and the ORACLE found (4,9) — the
# `34-56` further along, which the leading `\b` is perfectly happy with. A
# no-match row has to have no match ANYWHERE in the subject, not just at the
# position the author was thinking about.)
n "912-34"
n "_12-34"
# ...and the same subject with the barrier removed must still match, so the
# `n` rows above cannot pass by refusing everything
m "a 12-34" 2 7

pattern \bat [a-z]
features assertions
n "bat x"
n "_at x"
n "9at x"
m "b at x" 2 6

# ---- §7 the NEGATIVE examples: patterns the selection DECLINES --------------
#
# These get no offset-k skip — three of them are the bench's own controls and
# one is its derivation-domain control. They are here so that a change which
# starts selecting them has to move a case that names them.
# tests/codegen/run_offset_skip.sh asserts the DECLINE on the artifact; these
# rows assert that the answers are what they are either way.

# every offset is the same 16-byte hex class: no selective position exists
pattern \b[0-9a-f]{8}\b
features classes,assertions
m "deadbeef" 0 8
n "deadbee"
n "deadbeeff"

# unequal-width alternation: the `.` sits at a VARIABLE offset
pattern (?:[0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}
features classes
m "10.0.0.1" 0 8
m "x 192.168.1.254 y" 2 15
n "10.0.0"

# a `+`-smeared prefix — the email patterns' shape, the derivation-domain
# control: `@` is at no fixed offset at all
pattern [a-z0-9._%+-]+@[a-z0-9.-]+
features classes
m "user.name@example.com" 0 21
m "<a@b.co>" 1 7
n "no at sign here"

# a one-byte pattern has no offset 1 to select
pattern :
m "a:b" 1 2
n "abc"
